      "body": "## Purpose\n\n`hc_pre` / `hc_post` in `vllm/model_executor/layers/mhc.py` are rank-agnostic — they read `shape[-2:]` as `(hc_mult, hidden_size)` and treat everything before it as outer dims. The two helpers that feed them hardcode rank 2:\n\n```python\ndef hc_expand(x, n):    return x.unsqueeze(1).expand(-1, n, -1).contiguous()\ndef hc_contract(x, n):  return x.mean(dim=1)\n```\n\n`unsqueeze(1)` only coincides with \"second-to-last\" for rank-2 input, and `expand` hardcodes three sizes. A model runner that passes bucketed rank-3 `[1, T, hidden]` or rank-4 `[bs, 1, seq, hidden]` activations — the Intel Gaudi backend does — hits:\n\n```\nRuntimeError: expand: the requested shape has too few dimensions!\n```\n\n`hc_contract` has the matching problem: for rank-3 input, `mean(dim=1)` reduces over the token axis rather than the mHC axis.\n\nFound while bringing up GLM-5.3-Flash on Intel Gaudi 2.\n\n## Test Plan\n\n`test_hc_expand_contract_rank_agnostic` in `tests/kernels/mhc/test_mhc_kernels.py`, CPU-only. Covers rank 2/3/4, checks that `hc_expand` puts the axis second-to-last, and verifies the round-trip `hc_contract(hc_expand(x)) == x` (exact, since `hc_expand` replicates).\n\n```bash\npytest tests/kernels/mhc/test_mhc_kernels.py -k rank_agnostic\n```\n\n## Test Result\n\nWith the fix, all three ranks pass:\n\n```\n3 passed\n```\n\nAgainst the unpatched code:\n\n```\nFAILED test_hc_expand_contract_rank_agnostic[4-shape1]   # rank-3\nFAILED test_hc_expand_contract_rank_agnostic[4-shape2]   # rank-4\n2 failed, 1 passed\n```\n\n**The rank-2 case passes both before and after** — that is the evidence CUDA paths are unaffected by this change.\n\n---\n\n*AI disclosure: this change was developed with assistance from Claude (Claude Code). I reviewed every changed line, reproduced the failure and validated the fix on real hardware, and ran the tests myself.*\n\n",
      "changedFiles": 2,
